WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  our series a more perfect union aims to  show that what unites us as americans  is far far greater than what divides us  this morning an er doctor reflects on  his own painful experiences with race  and his push for diversity in a sport  that he loves  dr omar amir is a former u.s olympic  water polo player  and among the first african americans to  represent our country in this sport  he wrote an essay sharing his story  about overcoming racism  to find success in the pool cbs this  morning saturday co-host dana jacobson  spoke with dr amir  about his work to promote inclusion and  understanding  take a deep breath in and hold it  out of his scrubs and in the pool at the  university of california irvine  er doctor omar ahmer is still fueled by  competition  his strength and speed made him stand  out as a student here in the 90s  amar recalls being a small guy with big  dreams  since i was seven or eight years old my  only dream in life was to be an olympian  he came to the campus on a swim  scholarship but wanted to try water polo  so armor asked the coach to give him a  shot  he laughed at me and i just got in the  water within five minutes one of the  seniors broke my nose  made me very well aware of where i was  at the coach was  in his uh most loving way was like you  know you're going to let this little  n-bomb  beat all of you guys and i wanted to  kill someone after that but i just  used that as more motivation to succeed  while armor was enraged by the slur he  fell  hard for the sport and tried to prove  his worth to the team  and himself you could look across the  pool and there was no one look like me  at one point my water polo coach told me  that dark-skinned people had  lighter bones and we'd be better at land  sports you know  in so many sports the great equalizer is  once you can play  no one cares about anything else not in  swimming and water polo  in 1996 he made the national team  despite enduring physical and emotional  intimidation  they just destroyed me in practice  just make it so miserable  that they'd hoped that i would quit i am  certain now that if i was white  none of this would have happened after  spending his undergraduate years  strengthening his body and mind  almer applied and was accepted to  harvard medical school  but he remained focused on his olympic  dream splitting his time between med  school and boston during the week  and water polo training some 3 000 miles  away in california on the weekends  armor made the 2004 team competing in  greece along with goalie janai kerr  the first two african americans to  represent the u.s  in the sport i remember turning over to  our goalie and being like  we're here wow i finally did it  did you enjoy it then i loved it i loved  it  last year as protesters filled the  streets demanding  social justice omer began to reflect on  how  his race and ethnicity impacted his life  he published an essay nearly every team  meal  included pork which as a muslim i could  not eat when i asked for different food  i was singled out and told it was this  or nothing  while armor was shunned by some for  speaking out the essay prompted a flood  of public speaking invitations he spoke  by zoom to employees at global marketing  firm in ocean  tim murphy is ceo i think it's really  opening up people's eyes  i want to make sure that all employees  understand  how how to treat people in the workplace  it was so impactful and so inspirational  i think that's really what people took  away  ashley johnson the first black woman on  the u.s  water polo roster will be tending goal  in japan  amer has coached and encouraged her  since she was a kid  it makes me want to fight harder it lets  me know that there's so much more change  that needs to happen in our sport so  your liver looks beautiful  no one ever looked at me and said oh  this guy is going to succeed they just  gave me a million reasons why i should  quit it was just  fuel to the fire for me i'm going to be  better and smarter  and stronger than all of these people  and someday they're going to give me  respect  the son of egyptian immigrants in his  essay alma writes  i am a dark-skinned curly-haired  southern californian  i am an american i do believe we have  the power to heal ourselves from the  poison of racism  [Music]  cbs this morning dana jacobson  huntington beach california  love everything about the doctor i mean  your heart goes out for the struggle  that he had  but also got to give a shout out to tim  murphy who saw that and said my  employees need to hear this  you know that's that's what i think all  this conversation is about that it will  touch someone in a way  that they will say you know what we need  to learn more about this right bravo  and he's turning the pain that he  experienced growing up in in this sport  into power to inspire  others and look at the you know the  athlete that they just highlighted who's  going to the olympics oh we're lucky in  many ways that he was as resilient as he  was  you know that he just kept going that's  true look at the stuff he had to take  yeah that shot where he's standing in  the picture and his head is hanging down  and everybody's looking that's pain  all right he made it yes he made it  doctor you made it

